{:kind :reply, :text "\n\n(define conv:history-source (behavior:source))\n(define conv:history (behavior:scan conv:history-source seq:concat (seq:empty)))\n(define conv:history-latest (behavior:latest-value conv:history (seq:empty)))\n\n(define conv:user-source (behavior:source))\n(define conv:user-events (behavior:map conv:user-source (network [rec] (event:point (data:get :id rec) (data:get :time rec) rec))))\n(-> user-ingress conv:user-source)\n\n(define conv:reply (network [assistant-record]\n  (let [delivered (message-to-user assistant-record)\n        ack-record (data:record :role \"assistant\" :content (data:get :content delivered))\n        _ (-> (event:point (data:get :id delivered) (data:get :time delivered) (seq:append (seq:empty) ack-record)) conv:history-source)]\n    delivered)))\n\n(define conv:system-prompt \"You are in a Lain conversation. Reply by calling (conv:reply (data:record :id \\\"assistant-N\\\" :time T :role \\\"assistant\\\" :content \\\"your reply\\\")) where T is the user time + 1 and N increments. Return only that executable Lain form.\")\n\n(define conv:build-request (network [user-record history-vector]\n  (let [user-msg (data:record :role \"user\" :content (data:get :content user-record))\n        system-msg (data:record :role \"system\" :content conv:system-prompt)\n        messages (seq:concat (seq:append (seq:empty) system-msg) history-vector (seq:append (seq:empty) user-msg))]\n    (data:record :id (text:format \"req-%s\" (data:get :id user-record)) :messages messages))))\n\n(define conv:request (conv:build-request user-ingress conv:history-latest))\n(define conv:model-response (model:request conv:request))", :thinking "Let me analyze this complex task. I need to construct a small live conversation network in Lain. Let me break down the requirements:\n\n1. Build history with temporal reducers (behavior:scan, behavior:source, etc.)\n2. Preserve chronological user messages and acknowledged assistant replies\n3. Connect user-ingress to model requests\n4. Define a reusable reply function that delivers to the user, then adds the acknowledged reply to history\n5. Subsequent requests must contain actual prior user messages and delivered assistant replies as role-tagged messages\n6. Provider thinking and generated source are never conversation replies\n7. Each subsequent model response must be executable Lain calling the reply function with a new assistant message\n8. Conversation requests should include an explicit system message containing the reply-function calling convention\n9. Do not include setup code or provider thinking in conversation history\n10. Use the same model throughout\n11. The parent binding `history` retains generated source code automatically; it is NOT conversation history\n12. Use a different name for the conversation accumulator\n13. memory-cell is a live compound interface with open writable slots\n14. Do not send a request until a user event arrives\n15. Unknown inputs suspend a computation; avoid cycles requiring a future reply before the first request\n16. model:request is a function and must be invoked, not used as a connection target\n17. Inspect diagnostics if construction fails\n\nKey constraints from the language-introduction-cell:\n- Lain is NOT Clojure\n- Return raw parenthesized forms only; no triple-backtick fences\n- A callable is invoked as `(f x)`; `(-> x f)` writes TO the callable cell and does NOT call f\n- Only binding/argument vectors use literal brackets; construct data vectors with seq:empty and seq:append\n- Temporal sources accept occurrences from event:point, never bare message records or other behavior objects\n- A scan takes its source as its first argument\n- behavior:scan, map, and event-filter produce behavior objects, not ordinary records or vectors\n- Use behavior:latest-value to project a selected behavior value before passing to ordinary primitives\n- An optional fallback makes an empty behavior usable without inventing an occurrence\n- seq:project projects one key, so `(seq:project :role xs)` returns role strings, not message records\n- Model messages must be records with string :role and :content\n- System instructions must be first\n- The user ingress is a message RECORD, not a behavior or callable\n- You must capture its occurrences with event:point if you need a temporal source\n- `(define name expression)` binds a cell\n- `(define pending)` declares an unknown cell\n- `(network [x y] expression)` defines a callable\n- `(let [x expression y expression] body)` binds locals\n- `(-> value target-cell)` connects cells\n- `(if condition yes no)` selects a branch\n- Qualified names avoid editor collisions\n- Use data:record for immutable plain records, NOT struct\n- `(data:record :key value)` constructs a record; `(data:get :key record)` reads it\n- seq:empty [] returns an empty vector; seq:append [vector element] appends one element\n- seq:concat [vector ...] concatenates vectors into one flat vector\n- seq:last [vector] reads the last element\n- seq:project [key vector] projects a key over a vector\n- data:equal? [a b] compares values\n- event:point [id numeric-time value] captures a retained temporal occurrence\n- Each identity is immutable: later changes at its input do not withdraw earlier occurrences\n- behavior:source [] creates a reducer cell; connect supported event:point results to it with ->\n- behavior:scan [source reducer initial] emits a state at EVERY ordered occurrence\n- behavior:map [behavior callable] maps values\n- behavior:event-filter [behavior predicate] keeps matching historical occurrences\n- behavior:latest-value [behavior optional-fallback] projects its most recent value\n- model:request [record] submits { :id string :messages vector-of-role-content-records }\n- user-ingress publishes a record with :id, :time (odd integer), :role user, :content\n- Assistant time should be user time + 1 and assistant id must differ from user id\n- message-to-user [record] acknowledges a record containing :id :time :role assistant :content\n- source:admit [complete-reply lexical-target support-cell-reference] admits complete forms sequentially\n- environment:overview [environment] lists visible binding titles\n- cell:reference [cell] yields an inspectable reference\n- inspect:cell [reference] exposes :edge, :value, :inputs, :outputs\n- cell:value [cell] connects that edge's strongest value directly to an ordinary cell\n- data:snapshot [compound] resolves a finite compound into a plain record\n\nNow, the critical challenge: I need to build a conversation loop where:\n1. user-ingress publishes a record (not a behavior)\n2. I need to capture user-ingress occurrences with event:point to feed a temporal source\n3. But user-ingress is a RECORD, not a behavior - so I need to figure out how to capture it\n\nWait, let me re-read: \"The user ingress is a message RECORD, not a behavior or callable. You must capture its occurrences with event:point if you need a temporal source.\"\n\nSo user-ingress is a cell that holds a record. To make it temporal, I need to use event:point on it. But event:point takes [id numeric-time value]. The value would be the record from user-ingress.\n\nActually, looking more carefully at the construction-review-cell: \"A bare acknowledged record must be converted to a temporal occurrence before feeding a temporal source.\"\n\nAnd: \"Event identity and time must vary with actual events; constants user/1 and assistant/2 cannot identify three turns.\"\n\nSo I need to use the :time from the record as the event time, and the :id as the event identity.\n\nLet me think about the architecture:\n\n1. **User ingress capture**: user-ingress is a cell holding a record. I need to capture its occurrences. Since it's a record (not a behavior), I need to use event:point with the record's :id and :time.\n\n2. **Conversation history**: A behavior:scan that accumulates messages (both user and assistant) as a vector of role-content records.\n\n3. **Model request**: When a user message arrives, construct a request with system message + history, call model:request.\n\n4. **Reply function**: A network that takes an assistant message record, calls message-to-user to deliver it, then adds the acknowledged reply to history.\n\n5. **The loop**: user event → build request → model responds → reply function delivers and updates history → next user event uses updated history.\n\nThe tricky part is avoiding cycles. The construction-review-cell warns: \"avoid cycles requiring a future reply before the first request.\"\n\nLet me think about how to structure this without cycles:\n\n- user-ingress is a cell that gets written to externally\n- I need to detect when user-ingress changes and trigger a model request\n- The model request needs the current history\n- The reply function needs to update history\n\nThe challenge: how do I trigger a model:request when user-ingress changes? model:request is a function that must be invoked. I can't just connect user-ingress to it.\n\nOne approach: Use a behavior:source for user events, scan to build history, and then... but model:request needs to be called, not connected.\n\nWait, let me re-read the constraints more carefully. \"model:request is a function and must be invoked, not used as a connection target.\"\n\nSo I need to actually call model:request. But in a propagator network, how do I trigger a function call based on incoming data?\n\nLooking at the examples, model:request is called like: `(model:request (data:record :id \"...\" :messages ...))`\n\nIn a propagator network, if I write to a cell that's used as an argument to model:request, the function should re-evaluate. But the constraint says it must be \"invoked, not used as a connection target.\"\n\nHmm, let me think about this differently. In Lain's propagator model:\n- `(define result (model:request input-cell))` - this creates a network where result depends on input-cell. When input-cell gets a value, model:request is called.\n\nActually wait - \"model:request is a function and must be invoked, not used as a connection target.\" This means I can't do `(-> something model:request)`. But I CAN do `(define result (model:request something))` which invokes it.\n\nSo the pattern would be:\n```\n(define request-payload (build-request user-message history))\n(define model-response (model:request request-payload))\n```\n\nWhen user-message changes, request-payload changes, and model:request is re-invoked.\n\nBut there's a cycle problem: history depends on model-response (through the reply function), and model-response depends on history (through the request). This is the cycle the construction-review-cell warns about.\n\nTo break the cycle: The history should be a behavior:scan that accumulates. The model request should use the LATEST value of history (via behavior:latest-value), not the current scan state directly.\n\nActually, let me think about this more carefully with the temporal model:\n\n1. user-ingress publishes a record → I capture it as an event:point → feeds a behavior:source\n2. A behavior:scan accumulates all messages (user + assistant) into a vector\n3. When a new user event arrives, I need to call model:request with", :finish-reason "stop", :truncated? false}